---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Math from "../components/Math.astro";

const tests = [
  {
    code: "ADF",
    name: "Augmented Dickey-Fuller",
    window: "Full sample, once.",
    tex: String.raw`\Delta y_t = \mu + \delta y_{t-1} + \sum_{i=1}^{k} \phi_i \Delta y_{t-i} + \varepsilon_t
      \qquad H_0\!:\ \delta = 0 \quad\text{vs}\quad H_1\!:\ \delta > 0`,
    body: "The regression is the ordinary ADF test, but the alternative is right-tailed instead of left: a standard unit-root test asks whether δ < 0 (the series is stationary), bubble testing asks whether δ > 0 — whether the series is mildly explosive. Run once over the whole sample, ADF has no way to localize *when* that explosiveness happened, and its power collapses once a bubble has collapsed back to trend by the end of the sample.",
  },
  {
    code: "SADF",
    name: "Supremum ADF",
    window: "Origin fixed at 0. End r₂ grows from r₀ to 1.",
    tex: String.raw`\mathrm{SADF} = \sup_{r_2 \in [r_0,\,1]} \mathrm{ADF}_{0}^{\,r_2}`,
    body: "Phillips, Wu & Yu (2011). Re-runs the ADF regression on an expanding window that always starts at the beginning of the sample, and takes the largest statistic across all window endpoints. This localizes a single bubble reasonably well — but because the origin never moves, a second, later bubble gets diluted by all the stable pre-bubble data still sitting in every window, and SADF loses power.",
  },
  {
    code: "GSADF",
    name: "Generalized SADF",
    window: "Both r₁ and r₂ float: r₂ ∈ [r₀,1], r₁ ∈ [0, r₂−r₀].",
    tex: String.raw`\mathrm{GSADF} = \sup_{\substack{r_2 \in [r_0,\,1] \\ r_1 \in [0,\,r_2 - r_0]}} \mathrm{ADF}_{r_1}^{\,r_2}`,
    body: "Phillips, Shi & Yu (2015). Letting the window's start float too, not just its end, is the whole fix: the supremum is now taken over every window that could plausibly contain a bubble, so an early bubble no longer contaminates the test for a later, separate one. This is the headline test in exuber, and the one almost everything else in the suite is built to compute or calibrate.",
  },
  {
    code: "BSADF",
    name: "Backward SADF",
    window: "r₂ fixed at each point in time. r₁ ∈ [0, r₂−r₀].",
    tex: String.raw`\mathrm{BSADF}_{r_2} = \sup_{r_1 \in [0,\,r_2 - r_0]} \mathrm{ADF}_{r_1}^{\,r_2}`,
    body: "GSADF's supremum, but stopped one step early: instead of also taking the sup over r₂, BSADF reports one statistic per time point r₂. That gives a full time series — the thing exuber actually plots — and GSADF is simply the maximum of this series. It's also what makes date-stamping possible.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Methodology — exuber"
  description="The recursive right-tailed unit root test family: ADF, SADF, GSADF, and BSADF, and how they date-stamp bubbles."
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6">
    <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-explosive">Methodology</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl leading-[1.15] text-ink">Four tests, one recursion.</h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
        An asset price is <em class="text-ink not-italic">explosive</em> when it grows faster than a
        random walk can explain — the autoregressive root of the series exceeds one. That's the
        signature of a bubble while it's inflating, and it's directly testable: run the same
        right-tailed unit-root regression over the right window, repeatedly, and read off where the
        statistic crosses its threshold.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="flex flex-col gap-14 border-t border-border py-16">
      {tests.map((t, i) => (
        <article class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-[80px_1fr]">
          <div class="font-mono text-xs text-muted">
            <div class="text-ink">{t.code}</div>
            <div class="mt-1">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")} / 04</div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-ink">{t.name}</h2>
            <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">{t.window}</p>
            <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded border border-border bg-panel px-4 py-3 text-ink">
              <Math tex={t.tex} display />
            </div>
            <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{t.body}</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-border py-16 sm:py-20">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">The recursive window</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-3xl text-ink">Where r₁ and r₂ live.</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        r₀ is the minimum window fraction — too short a window and the ADF regression has no power.
        Every valid GSADF window is a point in the shaded region below: an end r₂ no earlier than
        r₀, and a start r₁ no later than r₂ − r₀. The dashed slice is the window BSADF uses to
        produce a single reading at r₂*.
      </p>

      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 240" class="mt-10 w-full max-w-md" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of the feasible (r1, r2) window region: a right triangle from r0 to 1, with a vertical slice at r2* showing the BSADF window">
        <!-- axes -->
        <line x1="40" y1="200" x2="300" y2="200" stroke="var(--color-border)" stroke-width="1" />
        <line x1="40" y1="200" x2="40" y2="20" stroke="var(--color-border)" stroke-width="1" />
        <text x="34" y="24" text-anchor="end" font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-muted)">r₁</text>

        <!-- feasible region: triangle (r0,0) (1,0) (1,1-r0) -->
        <polygon points="76,200 300,200 300,60" fill="var(--color-stable)" opacity="0.12" />
        <polygon points="76,200 300,200 300,60" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-stable)" stroke-width="1.25" />

        <!-- r0 tick -->
        <line x1="76" y1="200" x2="76" y2="206" stroke="var(--color-muted)" stroke-width="1" />
        <text x="76" y="219" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-muted)">r₀</text>
        <text x="300" y="219" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-muted)">1</text>
        <text x="170" y="236" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-muted)">r₂ (window end)</text>

        <!-- BSADF slice at r2* -->
        <line x1="210" y1="200" x2="210" y2="106" stroke="var(--color-explosive)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3" />
        <circle cx="210" cy="106" r="2.5" fill="var(--color-explosive)" />
        <text x="215" y="196" text-anchor="start" font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="9.5" fill="var(--color-explosive)">r₂*</text>
        <text x="160" y="34" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-explosive)">BSADF(r₂*) = sup over the dashed slice</text>
      </svg>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-border py-16 sm:py-20">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Date-stamping</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-3xl text-ink">From a statistic to a calendar date.</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Plot BSADF(r₂) against its (r₂-specific) critical value across the whole sample and the
        crossings are the bubble dates: origination is the first r₂ where the statistic climbs
        above the threshold, termination is the first later r₂ where it falls back below. Phillips,
        Shi &amp; Yu require the excursion to last at least <Math tex={String.raw`\delta \log(T)/T`} /> of
        the sample before it counts, so a one-period spike above the line isn't mistaken for a bubble.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
